  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1242 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nA change from perpetual heat has finally loosened its grasp\nacross the Ohio River Valley and Mid-South today. Surface\nobservations place a cold front as far south as northern portions\nof the Gulf. Aloft, a shortwave trough will traverse the Middle\nMississippi River Valley and into the Ohio River Valley through\ntoday. Mid-level WV imagery shows a large area of subsidence over\nthe region, which will likely keep the clouds through at least\nthe early afternoon hours and keep surface temperatures from\nrising too high. Therefore, expect highs today in the low 80s\nwith some areas potentially remaining below 80 degrees with lows\nin the upper 50s and low 60s.\n\nThe current surface pattern will largely continue through the end\nof the week and into this weekend. An upper ridge will eventually\nbuild over the southern Plains, bringing southerlies back to the\nregion by the end of this weekend. Warmer temperatures will\narrive as a result, but highs are expected to only reach up into\nthe middle and upper 80s with NBM probabilities of reaching at or\nabove 90 degrees only making it to around 30% - 40% next Tuesday.\nSome rain is possible as some guidance depicts a few straggling\nshowers making their way southwest into eastern Arkansas Sunday\nmorning. However, PoPs remain below 40% owing to relatively high\nforecast uncertainty in the evolution of the antecedent\nprecipitation Saturday evening. Regardless, the majority of the\nforecast is likely to be dry, especially through late this\nweekend.\n\nLooking beyond into early next week reveals ever-increasing\nforecast uncertainty as Hurricane Polo, currently present a few\nhundred miles off the southern coastline of Mexico, recurves\nnorth into the mid-latitude westerlies. The interaction between\nPolo and a trough over the Pacific Coastline will be important to\nthe forecast beyond Sunday as it phases with the trough. NBM data\nfrom this morning reflects this fact with a 15 - 20 degree spread\namongst forecast 25th - 75th high and low temperatures. However,\nthe prevailing average amongst the ensembles is for the resulting\nupper trough structure to move east across the CONUS alongside a\nlow chance for 590+ dm 500 mb heights to return. Regardless, it\nis likely still too early to deduce a reasonable forecast into\nnext week until model variability settles down in the coming\ndays.",
